@UNIX
Unix-style commands on the TFR/911H (try "man <name>"):
  ls ll cat cp mv rm mkdir rmdir pwd cd ps df less man
  grep clear uname exit vi vim info   and: tetris zm beep click
FujiNet ("man fujinet"): fnconfig fnweather fnnews fnstocks fnbounce ...
Most are aliases that run the OS-9 command shown by "man".
OS-9 paths use / like Unix; /DD is the disk, . and .. work.
Differences: pipes are "!" not "|"; ">>" redirects errors (there is no
append); "<>" and ">>>" redirect several paths; "&" runs in background.
@LS
Syntax: ls [-l] [<path>]   (runs: dir, with -l as -e)
Usage : List a directory.  "ls -l" or "ll" gives the long form.
@LL
Syntax: ll [<path>]        (runs: dir -e)
Usage : List a directory, long form.
@CAT
Syntax: cat <path>         (runs: list)
Usage : Print a text file.
@CP
Syntax: cp <from> <to>     (runs: copy)
Usage : Copy a file.
@MV
Syntax: mv <path> <newname>   (runs: rename)
Usage : Rename a file or directory.  It cannot move it to another directory.
@RM
Syntax: rm <path> ...      (runs: del)
Usage : Delete files.
@MKDIR
Syntax: mkdir <path>       (runs: makdir)
Usage : Make a directory.
@RMDIR
Syntax: rmdir <path>       (runs: deldir)
Usage : Delete a directory and what is in it (it asks first).
@PWD
Syntax: pwd
Usage : Print the data (working) directory.  pxd prints the execution directory.
@CD
Syntax: cd <path>          (Shell built-in, same as chd)
Usage : Change the data (working) directory.  chx changes the execution
        directory, where commands are found.
@PS
Syntax: ps [-e]            (runs: procs)
Usage : List processes.  -e lists everyone's.
@DF
Syntax: df [/dd]           (runs: free)
Usage : Show free space on a disk.  mfree shows free memory.
@LESS
Syntax: less <path>        (runs: more)
Usage : Show a file a screen at a time.
@MAN
Syntax: man [<command>]    (runs: help)
Usage : Show help for a command.  "man unix" lists the Unix-style commands.
@GREP
Syntax: grep [-i] [-n] <pattern> [<path>]
Usage : Print lines containing the pattern (a plain string, not a regexp).
        -i ignores case, -n shows line numbers.  Reads standard input
        if no path is given, so "dir ! grep foo" works.
@CLEAR
Syntax: clear
Usage : Clear the screen.
@UNAME
Syntax: uname
Usage : Say what this is.
@EXIT
Syntax: exit               (Shell built-in)
Usage : Leave this shell.
@TETRIS
Syntax: tetris
Usage : Play Tetris.  Arrows or a/d move, up or w rotates, down or s drops
        a row, space drops the piece, p pauses, q quits, m toggles music.
@VI
Syntax: vi [-w <cols>] [-h <rows>] [<file>]
Usage : A small vi.  ESC returns to normal mode.  Normal mode:
        move   h j k l arrows w b e W B E 0 ^ $ G gg H M L f t F T ; , %
               { } ^F ^B ^D ^U PgUp PgDn, /text ?text n N *
        insert i a I A o O     change x X D C s S r J ~ p P >> << u .
        d c y > < + motion (dw c$ y3j dG d/text), dd cc yy, counts
        ZZ saves and quits, ^G shows the file, ^L redraws.
        :w [file] :q :q! :wq :x :e[!] [file] :r file :N :$
        :[range]s/old/new/[g] :[range]d   (range: N  .  $  %  N,M)
        Searches are plain text.  Files are written with CR line ends.
@VIM
Syntax: vim [<file>]         (runs: vi)
Usage : The same as vi.
@INFO
Syntax: info [-p]
Usage : Show the system: OS version and settings, memory, processes,
        devices, disks (size and free space), interrupt handlers, the
        modules in memory, and the TFR/911H hardware's own report.
        -p gives plain text, with no bold headings.
@ZM
Syntax: zm [-w <cols>] [-h <rows>] [-m <kbytes>] [-d] <story>
Usage : Play an Infocom (or Inform) version 3 story file, such as
        zm zork1.  A bare name is also looked for in /DD/GAMES, and .z3
        may be left off.  -w and -h give the terminal size (80x24); -d is
        for a dumb terminal (no status line).  save and restore ask for a
        file name, in the current data directory.
@BEEP
Syntax: beep
Usage : Ring the bell: the terminal's, and the TFR/911H's speaker.
@CLICK
Syntax: click on|off
Usage : Turn the TFR/911H's keyclicks on or off.
@FUJINET
FujiNet commands on the TFR/911H.  Slots count from 0: device slot 0
is /X0 (the FujiNet's slot 1).  Host slot 1 is often its SD card.
  fnconfig         FujiNet CONFIG: status, hosts, devices, WiFi
  fnweather        the weather where you are, and the week's
  fnwx             the first fnweather: the weather now, in words
  fnnews           the news, from the FujiNet news server
  fnstocks         a stock ticker, from Finnhub
  fnbounce         Bounce World, shapes shared with other computers
  fnstatus         firmware version, WiFi network and IP settings
  fnlisthosts      the eight host slots
  fngethost  <host_slot>            show one host slot
  fnsethost  <host_slot> <host>     set one host slot
  fnmount    <host_slot>            mount a host
  fnlistdevs       the four device slots, /X0-/X3
  fngetdevfile <device_slot>        show a device slot's image
  fnsetdevfile <device_slot> <host_slot> <mode> <path>
                   put an image in a device slot (mode 1 read, 2 write)
  fnmountimg <device_slot> [<mode>] mount a device slot's image
At TCL>, l2fn boots NitrOS-9 from device slot 0 (/DD is then /X0).
@FNCONFIG
Syntax: fnconfig
Usage : FujiNet CONFIG.  1 Status, 2 Hosts, 3 Devices, 4 WiFi, I info,
        ? help, Q quit.  On Hosts: up/down, E edit, M mount, U unmount.
        On Devices: up/down, B browse the host, E edit the path, M mount,
        U eject.  On WiFi: S scan, E enter an SSID.  Leave /X0 alone
        while it is /DD.
@FNWEATHER
Syntax: fnweather
Usage : The weather where you are, now and for the week, from ip-api.com
        and Open-Meteo, with pictures.  R fetch now, F the forecast (N
        and B turn its pages, W comes back), U units, L the place (a
        city, or Enter for where you are), I info, Q or Esc quit.  Kept
        in /DD/SYS/fnweather.dat; fetched again after half an hour.
@FNWX
Syntax: fnwx
Usage : The first fnweather, in assembly: the weather now, in words.
        R refresh, U units, L locate again, I info, ? help, D the data
        received, Q quit.
@FNNEWS
Syntax: fnnews
Usage : The news, from the FujiNet news server.  Up/down choose a topic or
        a story, Enter reads it, left/right turn the page of stories, G
        goes to one.  In a story, up/down turn its pages and I shows its
        date and source.  I at the topics: about.  Esc goes back, quits.
@FNSTOCKS
Syntax: fnstocks
Usage : Ten stocks, with a ticker, from Finnhub; the FujiNet keeps the
        list.  Arrows or 1-9 and 0 select, E edit, D delete, L look up a
        name, I company information, R refresh, H hide and S show the
        list, Esc quit.  Quotes are fetched again every five minutes.
@FNBATTLESHIP
Syntax: fnbattleship
Usage : Fuji Battleship: sea battle for up to four players, people or
        the server's robots, through the FujiNet.  Tables: arrows and
        Space join, R refresh, H how to play, N name, S sound, Q quit.
        At a table Space is ready; placing ships, arrows move, R turns
        and Space places; on your turn, arrows or a letter A-J and a
        digit 1-0 aim and Space fires.  Esc is the menu (Q leaves).
@FNBOUNCE
Syntax: fnbounce
Usage : Bounce World: shapes bouncing across the screens of all the
        clients of a server.  On the title screen, S sets the server
        (host:9003), N your name, and W the width (40: round shapes,
        80: twice the room); any other key goes on.  In the world:
        I info, W who, 1-5 add a shape, +/- speed, F freeze, R reset
        (these change the world for everyone), C reverse video, L
        flash and S sound on collisions, Q or Esc quit.
@FNSTATUS
Syntax: fnstatus
Usage : The FujiNet's firmware version, host name, WiFi network and IP
        settings, and whether WiFi is connected.
@FNLISTHOSTS
Syntax: fnlisthosts
Usage : List the FujiNet's eight host slots (0-7).
@FNGETHOST
Syntax: fngethost <host_slot>
Usage : Show one host slot.
@FNSETHOST
Syntax: fnsethost <host_slot> <host>
Usage : Set one host slot (a TNFS server's name, or SD), keeping the rest.
@FNMOUNT
Syntax: fnmount <host_slot>
Usage : Mount a host, so its files can be put in device slots.
@FNLISTDEVS
Syntax: fnlistdevs
Usage : List the four DriveWire device slots, /X0-/X3: host slot, mode
        (1 read, 2 write) and image.
@FNGETDEVFILE
Syntax: fngetdevfile <device_slot>
Usage : Show the image in one device slot.
@FNSETDEVFILE
Syntax: fnsetdevfile <device_slot> <host_slot> <mode> <path>
Usage : Put the image <path> on host <host_slot> in a device slot, read
        only (mode 1) or read/write (mode 2).  The FujiNet saves it.
        Then fnmountimg mounts it.  Example, for /X1:
          fnsetdevfile 1 1 2 /games/disk.dsk;  fnmountimg 1 2;  dir /x1
@FNMOUNTIMG
Syntax: fnmountimg <device_slot> [<mode>]
Usage : Mount the image in a device slot, read only (mode 1, the default)
        or read/write (mode 2).
